A poor young wife sells her beautiful hair to buy her husband a watch chain, only to learn he sold his watch to buy her combs, turning useless gifts into proof of deep love.

1. Christmas Poverty and Longing  (passages 1-1)
    Della has almost no money for Christmas but desperately wants to give her husband something
    worthy.
       -> Having almost no money makes what the couple still owns matter more and pushes Della
       to find another way to give.
2. Two Treasures and Resolve  (passages 2-2)
    The couple owns only two proud treasures, her long hair and his heirloom watch, and Della
    rushes out determined to act.
       -> Her sudden resolve sends her to give up the one treasure she can part with to honor
       the other.
3. Selling Hair for Chain  (passages 3-3)
    Della sells her hair to buy a fine platinum chain worthy of Jim’s watch and hurries home to
    fix her shorn hair.
       -> With the chain bought and her appearance changed, she must go home and face Jim hoping
       love survives the change.
4. Fearful Wait and Reassurance  (passages 4-5)
    Della fears Jim will find her shorn hair ugly, but he struggles to understand, reassures her
    of his love, and hands her a mysterious gift.
       -> His puzzling reaction and unopened gift demand an explanation of what he sacrificed in
       return.
5. Useless Gifts Wisest Love  (passages 6-6)
    Both gifts prove unusable because Jim sold his watch to buy her combs, and their foolish
    sacrifice is called the wisest love of all.
